    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d364">

                <pb facs="0470" n="388" sameAs="frus:frus1865p3/pg388"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Bigelow</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 94.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1865-03-28"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >March</hi> 28, 1865.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatch of the 14th of March, No. 55,
                    has been received, and I have read with much interest the papers which accompany
                    it, and which illustrate the disquietude now prevailing in Paris.</p>
                <p>Fortunately, I have in my despatch of 15th instant, No. 71, explained to you the
                    views and sentiments which our military and political situation suggests. We
                    want our national rights. We are not looking for ulterior, national advantages,
                    or aggrandizement, much less for occasions for retaliating in other forms of
                    hostility against foreign states. We are not propagandists, although we are
                    consistent in our political convictions.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD,</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Bigelow,</hi> Esq., <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
